---
import Slide from '@decks/templates/Slide.astro';

// The model Matt arrived at, placed last because it explains the segment
// they just watched rather than setting it up. Everything the panel saw
// lands in one of these: Code Architecture and Figma Libraries were
// Platform, Accessibility and Documentation were Artifacts, Adoption was
// Service and Go-to-market at once.
//
// The point said out loud: most people build the first layer and stop.
//
// Two versions of this slide were wrong before this one, both for the
// same reason. The first drew four filled, bordered, rounded boxes with
// a dashed Governance band down the side. The second kept the boxes and
// only shrank them. But this deck has no cards anywhere else: the
// accessibility audit, Next Steps, Key Decisions and Adoption are all
// type on black, so four grey rectangles read as imported from another
// deck however carefully they are spaced. Hairline rules between bands
// say "layers" at least as clearly and cost nothing.
//
// Sizes match Next Steps, which is the closest sibling in the deck: four
// items, each a label over a description. Nothing here earns bigger type
// than the slide that closes the Checklist segment.
//
// Governance came out (Matt, 2026-09-14). It had been a band crossing
// all four, on the argument that it is who may change what at which
// layer. Correct, and it still did not earn its place: it is a fifth
// idea competing with the four the slide is named for.
//
// One thing still told over this slide, and it no longer needs the word
// governance to land: SYSTEMS WITHIN SYSTEMS. Teams shipped their own
// libraries for what was not systemized yet and changed those freely,
// core was reviewed, and those libraries were the source material for
// what got promoted up when it earned it. That is layering one level
// out, which is why it belongs here.
//
// The other thing to say: EVERY PART OF EVERY LAYER HAS TO BE REMOVABLE.
// A new design tool, a better framework, React to Svelte. We deleted the
// whole html-sketchapp to Abstract to Sketch pipeline when Figma won,
// and would build it again, because manual sync does not scale. The
// reason that cost a pipeline instead of the system is slide 11: code
// was the source of truth, so the design tool was replaceable by
// construction.

const layers = [
	{
		name: 'Artifacts',
		items: 'Components, tokens, icons, fonts',
	},
	{
		name: 'Platform',
		items: 'Packaging, versioning, distribution, design-to-code sync, CI',
	},
	{
		name: 'Service',
		items: 'Office hours, design and code review, consults, migration support',
	},
	{
		name: 'Go-to-market',
		items: 'Roadshows, release announcements, training, champions, public roadmap',
	},
];
---

<Slide title="Anvil Design System" subtitle="Four Layers">
	<div class="layers">
		{
			layers.map((l, i) => (
				<div class="layer">
					<span class="num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
					<span class="text-strong text-3xl font-medium">{l.name}</span>
					<span class="text-subdued text-2xl">{l.items}</span>
				</div>
			))
		}
	</div>
</Slide>

<style>
	/* Three columns shared by every band, so the names and the items line
	   up down the slide rather than each band setting its own widths. */
	.layers {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: auto 260px minmax(0, 1fr);
		align-items: baseline;
	}

	.layer {
		display: grid;
		grid-column: 1 / -1;
		grid-template-columns: subgrid;
		align-items: baseline;
		column-gap: 40px;
		padding: 28px 0;
		border-top: 1px solid rgb(156 163 175 / 0.2);
	}

	/* No rule above the first band. A leading rule reads as the underside
	   of something that is not there. */
	.layer:first-child {
		border-top: 0;
		padding-top: 0;
	}

	.layer:last-child {
		padding-bottom: 0;
	}

	/* The count does the work the title claims. Quiet enough to be a
	   rhythm down the left edge rather than a column of data. */
	.num {
		font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
		font-size: 18px;
		font-weight: 500;
		color: rgb(156 163 175 / 0.45);
	}
</style>
